# v24 — Japanese manuscript revision, LLM4Mat-Bench citation, three-part submission package
Date: 2026-06-02 (UTC)
Branch: devin/1789279210-ja-abstract-prose-trim (base: main after PR #581 merge)
Japanese freeze tag: ja-final -> 119230bb (moved from f80ac54c; two moves in this branch, 20c10308 then 119230bb)

## Scope decisions (author)
- Manuscript text only. Numbers, SSOT (paper/paper_data.json), saved model outputs, gold SQL, expected
  results, evaluation code and the six figure PNGs are unchanged from v23e.
- No English manuscript yet (procedure Phase 5+ not started). No paid LLM re-evaluation.

## 1. Manuscript changes (paper/stam-m_ja.tex), 30 -> 29 pages
- Abstract shortened to about half; the only quantitative value kept is the main execution recall (86.1%).
  Values removed from the Abstract (generalization A–D, independent-design 81.5%, paired JA/EN 92.0/90.0%,
  non-translated EN25 96.5%) remain in the main text and tables, so the number audit is unaffected.
- Metaphors, filler modifiers and emphasis words removed throughout ("核心", "大幅に", "真に", "劇的",
  "著しく", "顕著", "堅牢", "決定的に", "豊富な" etc.); redundant preambles compressed
  (103,876 -> ~97,800 characters before the DFT/plain-language pass).
- Abstract sentence 1 and the first paragraph of the Introduction now state that the target is a normalized RDB
  holding DFT-derived inorganic-materials data (OQMD / Materials Project / AFLOW / NOMAD); AtomWork-Adv is
  distinguished as a literature/experimental database. Abbreviations (RDB, DFT, FK, JOIN, few-shot, CTE,
  ablation) are explained at first use; long sentences split. Tone: plain and descriptive.
- Data-origin statement preserved: only the 89 pure-element reference energies are OQMD DFT values; compound and
  verification records are programmatically generated synthetic data (Methods, Limitations, Acknowledgements,
  supplementary tables).
- Related work: Rubungo et al., LLM4Mat-Bench (Mach. Learn.: Sci. Technol. 6, 020501, 2025;
  doi:10.1088/2632-2153/add3bb) added to references.bib (key rubungo2025llmmatbench) and cited in two places,
  both deliberately limited in scope:
  * Introduction: general-purpose LLMs applied to materials tasks by prompting alone show hallucinations /
    invalid outputs and need task-specific knowledge — reported for property prediction as well.
  * §LLM-only baseline: "the task differs, but the observation that materials-domain-specific errors remain in
    general-purpose LLM output agrees with the property-prediction benchmark".
  Not claimed: that prompt engineering is useless, or that LLM-only approaches fail (the LLM-only baseline here
  reaches 78.3%). \cite count 45 -> 47; figure/table/label/includegraphics counts unchanged
  (figure 5, table 26, includegraphics 6, label 96).
- Cover letter (Japanese draft) added: paper/cover_letter_ja.md (placeholders in [ ] for the author).

